course: biology
lesson: 131
title: Ice Age: real sabre-tooths, ground sloths, glyptodonts and dodos
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The film Ice Age is a cartoon, but many of its animals really lived: sabre-toothed cats, woolly mammoths, woolly rhinos, giant ground sloths and armoured glyptodonts.
explain: Picture a museum hall with a cat skull whose two fangs are as long as your school ruler: that is Smilodon, the real Diego, and its bones were pulled out of a sticky tar pit.
explain: The film mixes things up for fun: a real sabre-tooth would have hunted a young mammoth, not walked beside it, and dodos lived on one warm island far from any ice.
explain: The Ice Age was not ice everywhere either: great ice sheets lay over the north, while grassland, forest and desert covered much of the rest of the world.
fact: Diego's real kind is Smilodon, an extinct cat whose two upper fangs could be about 28 centimetres long; it was not a tiger but a separate, extinct branch of the cat family.
fact: Smilodon could open its jaws about 120 degrees, much wider than a lion, so the long fangs did not get in the way of biting, and its bite was weaker than a lion's because the long fangs could snap on bone.
fact: Thousands of Smilodon skeletons were found in the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles: plant-eaters got stuck in the tar and the hunters that came to eat them got stuck too.
fact: Some Smilodon skeletons show healed injuries, so scientists think they may have lived in groups, though this is still argued about.
fact: Some ground sloths were enormous: Megatherium was as big as an elephant, stood on its back legs to reach leaves and walked on the sides of its clawed feet.
fact: Ground sloths came from South America, spread north when the two Americas joined, and died out over 10,000 years ago, except on a few Caribbean islands where they lasted longer.
fact: Scrat is invented, but in 2011 scientists described a tiny long-fanged fossil mammal from about 94 million years ago in South America that reminded them of him.
fact: Real squirrels bury nuts and forget many of them, so the nuts grow into new trees.
fact: Glyptodonts were armadillo relatives as big as a small car, covered in a bony shell that protected them from hunters such as Smilodon.
fact: Dodos were big flightless birds that lived only on Mauritius in the Indian Ocean, with no predators and no fear of people, and they died out in the 1600s, about a hundred years after sailors arrived.
fact: Woolly rhinos had two horns, a long one at the front and a shorter one behind, and shaggy fur; they were painted in the Chauvet cave in France more than 30,000 years ago.
fact: In the Ice Age so much water was frozen into ice sheets that the sea was much lower, and people could walk from Asia to America over land that is now under the sea.
exercise: What is the real name of the sabre-toothed cat like Diego? | Smilodon | Sabre tiger | Megatherium | Glyptodon
exercise: About how long could a Smilodon fang be? | About 28 centimetres, as long as a ruler | About 3 centimetres, as long as a thumbnail | About 1 metre, as long as a cricket bat | About 8 centimetres, as long as a finger
exercise: Was Smilodon a kind of tiger? | No, it was a separate extinct branch of the cat family | Yes, it was a striped tiger with very long teeth, like Diego | Yes, it was the grandparent of all tigers | No, it was a kind of wolf
exercise: Why did Smilodon need to open its jaws so wide? | So its long fangs would not get in the way of biting | So it could roar much louder than any lion on the plains | So it could swallow mammoths whole | So it could cool down on hot days
exercise: How strong was Smilodon's bite compared with a lion's? | Weaker, because the long fangs could break on bone | Much stronger, strong enough to crush rocks and bones to dust | Exactly the same as a lion's | Ten times stronger than a lion's
exercise: Where were thousands of Smilodon skeletons found? | In the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles | Frozen deep in the thick ice of Antarctica, under the snow | In a volcano in Iceland | On the island of Mauritius
exercise: Why did a tar pit trap so many hunters? | They came to eat stuck animals and got stuck too | Hunters liked to drink the tar | The sticky tar smelled like fresh meat to them, so they ate it | Hunters slept in tar to keep warm
exercise: How big was the ground sloth Megatherium? | As big as an elephant | As big as a cat | As big as a sheep in a field | As big as a mouse
exercise: Where did ground sloths first come from? | South America | The North Pole | Australia | Europe
exercise: Is Scrat a real animal? | No, but a real tiny fossil mammal that reminded scientists of him was found | Yes, it was a real squirrel of the Ice Age that chased a single acorn | Yes, it still lives in Canada | No, and no animal like him was ever found
exercise: What were glyptodonts? | Armadillo relatives as big as a small car, with a bony shell | Giant tortoises with soft shells that swam in the cold seas | Small dinosaurs with armour | Huge beetles of the Ice Age
exercise: Where did real dodos live? | Only on the island of Mauritius | On the ice sheets of the north | All over Europe and Asia | In the forests of South America
exercise: Why did dodos have no fear of people? | They had lived with no predators on their island | People had fed them every day, so they thought people were friends | They were too big to be hurt | They could fly away at any moment
exercise: How many horns did a woolly rhino have? | Two, a long one at the front and a shorter one behind | One short horn on the forehead, like a unicorn | Four, two on each side | Three, in a row along its nose
exercise: Where did Ice Age people paint woolly rhinos? | In the Chauvet cave in France | In the La Brea Tar Pits | On the walls of Egyptian pyramids | On rocks in Mauritius
exercise: Was the whole world covered in ice in the Ice Age? | No, there were also grasslands, forests and deserts | Yes, every bit of land and sea was buried under thick ice | Yes, even the seas froze solid | No, there was no ice at all
exercise: Why was the sea lower in the Ice Age? | So much water was frozen in ice sheets on land | The Moon pulled the water away to the other side of the Earth | Mammoths drank a lot of it | The sea floor rose up everywhere
exercise: What would a real Smilodon have done with a young mammoth? | Hunted it as food | Travelled with it as a friend | Ignored it, it ate only fish | Carried it on its back
test: «3 mammoths n 2 sloths in the herd, how many trunks» | 3 | 5 | 2 | 6
test: A museum shows 4 Smilodon skulls. How many long sabre fangs are there in all? | 8 | 4 | 16 | 12
test: «my mate says Diego's just a tiger with big teeth» What is right? | No, Smilodon was not a tiger but a separate extinct branch of cats | Yes, all sabre-tooths were tigers | Yes, and tigers still grow fangs that long when they get very old | No, Diego was a kind of bear
test: In The Flintstones a sabre-toothed cat is a family pet who sleeps in a cave home with people. What is real? | Sabre-tooths were wild hunters of big plant-eaters, never pets | People kept sabre-tooths as pets in caves | Sabre-tooths only ate vegetables | Sabre-tooths lived with dinosaurs and people in the same caves
test: «Sid's a sloth so ground sloths were tiny and lived in trees, obvs» What is right? | Some were as big as elephants and far too heavy to climb | Yes, they were tiny and lived in trees | Yes, they hung upside down from branches like today's sloths | No, they lived under the sea
test: «dodos lived in the ice age snow, saw it in the film» What is right? | No, dodos lived only on warm Mauritius, far from the ice | Yes, they lived on the ice sheets next to mammoths and sloths | Yes, they waddled next to mammoths | No, dodos lived in Antarctica
test: What would happen to a tar pit full of stuck deer? | Hungry hunters would come and get stuck too | The deer would swim out easily once the sun warmed the tar | The tar would turn into water | Nothing, no animal would come near
test: «why was the dodo so easy to catch, dumb bird or what» What is right? | It had no predators on its island, so it never learned to fear people | It was far too heavy to walk, so it just sat and waited to be picked up | It was blind | It wanted to be caught
test: If the ice sheets of the Ice Age melted back into the sea, what would happen to the land bridge between Asia and America? | It would go under the sea | It would get wider | It would turn into ice | Nothing would change
test: «squirrels forget where they bury nuts lol useless» Why is that useful for the forest? | Forgotten nuts grow into new trees | Forgotten nuts turn into stones | Nothing grows from a buried nut, it just rots away | The nuts feed the worms only
test: «how did glyptodonts stay safe from sabre-tooths, they slow» What is right? | Their thick bony shell protected them | They ran faster than any cat, even with their heavy shells | They climbed tall trees | They hid under the ice
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the taxonomic position of Smilodon. | It constituted a separate and extinct branch of the cat family, distinct from the tiger | It was an ancestral form of the modern tiger, from which all tigers have descended | It was a member of the bear family | It was a large species of wolf
exam: Identify the principal reason for which great numbers of predatory animals were preserved in the La Brea Tar Pits. | Predators attracted to ensnared herbivores became ensnared themselves | Predators deliberately entered the tar in order to rest | The tar was consumed by predators as food during hard winters when prey was scarce | Predators were driven into the tar by human hunters
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the natural range of the dodo? | It was confined to the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean | It occupied the ice sheets of the northern hemisphere | It was distributed throughout Europe and Asia | It inhabited the forests of South America
exam: Ascertain the cause of the lowered sea level which prevailed during the Ice Age. | A great volume of water was retained in the form of ice sheets upon the land | The gravitational attraction of the Moon was greatly diminished | The sea floor was elevated throughout the world | The oceans were consumed by evaporation into space
